WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.645 --> 00:00:03.365
Function and organization questions, also known

2
00:00:03.705 --> 00:00:06.565
as RC method or method on steroids.

3
00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:10.845
And so all of the method, uh, question strategies

4
00:00:10.845 --> 00:00:13.205
that we know from LR really can come

5
00:00:13.205 --> 00:00:15.245
to help us here on function and organization questions.

6
00:00:15.245 --> 00:00:19.085
And RC because at its core we're asking the same sort

7
00:00:19.105 --> 00:00:20.805
of question in a method question

8
00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:23.445
as we are in these question types in rc, which is

9
00:00:23.835 --> 00:00:26.685
what did this piece of the text do for the author?

10
00:00:26.905 --> 00:00:28.725
And so in a function question on rc,

11
00:00:28.725 --> 00:00:31.125
what they're gonna do is locate a certain piece of text,

12
00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:33.365
maybe it's a word, a sentence, a paragraph.

13
00:00:33.505 --> 00:00:35.685
It could vary how much text they're grabbing onto.

14
00:00:35.905 --> 00:00:39.205
And they're gonna ask, okay, what did that piece of text do

15
00:00:39.545 --> 00:00:41.965
for the author in the context of their overall passage,

16
00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.245
their overall argument?

17
00:00:43.715 --> 00:00:46.325
What, what did this piece of text achieve?

18
00:00:46.705 --> 00:00:50.245
Um, and you wanna be using things like your method vocab

19
00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:52.565
list or the different skills that we've learned

20
00:00:52.565 --> 00:00:55.405
to describe the, like abstract function

21
00:00:55.465 --> 00:00:57.205
of text from logical reasoning.

22
00:00:57.455 --> 00:00:59.885
Those are the same sorts of phrases that we're going

23
00:00:59.885 --> 00:01:02.005
to be using to describe the function of text in RC

24
00:01:02.005 --> 00:01:04.605
because honestly, you can only describe the function

25
00:01:04.605 --> 00:01:06.645
of text in like pretty much one way.

26
00:01:06.985 --> 00:01:08.845
And it's the way that method answer choices

27
00:01:08.905 --> 00:01:10.045
are tending to be phrased.

28
00:01:10.105 --> 00:01:12.085
And so all of that work you've done with say,

29
00:01:12.085 --> 00:01:15.125
the method vocab remix in lr, it can really help you

30
00:01:15.205 --> 00:01:16.845
with coming up with your missions on

31
00:01:17.085 --> 00:01:18.125
function questions as well.

32
00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:20.325
Because the big difference between LR

33
00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:24.845
and rc, um, in terms of this area is the size

34
00:01:24.945 --> 00:01:26.565
of the text that you're taking into account.

35
00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:28.525
Of course on a method question on lr, you know,

36
00:01:28.525 --> 00:01:29.885
your SIM is only gonna be like that long.

37
00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:31.485
It's gonna be a couple of sentences max.

38
00:01:31.705 --> 00:01:35.365
And so you're gonna have a relatively contained project

39
00:01:35.825 --> 00:01:38.765
to examine what the function of different, um, pieces

40
00:01:38.785 --> 00:01:40.085
of text was overall.

41
00:01:40.145 --> 00:01:42.085
And of course this goes for argument part questions as well

42
00:01:42.085 --> 00:01:44.605
because you know, little piece, what did that do as well?

43
00:01:44.985 --> 00:01:48.645
Um, with RC of course we have a lot more text to work with

44
00:01:48.745 --> 00:01:50.445
and so since we have a lot more text to work with,

45
00:01:50.615 --> 00:01:52.325
we're going to have to be keeping track

46
00:01:52.385 --> 00:01:54.685
of multiple functions as we're going.

47
00:01:55.065 --> 00:01:57.725
Um, and I'm not saying that as you're translating,

48
00:01:57.725 --> 00:01:59.485
equipping, you have to know the function

49
00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.285
of every single piece of text as you're going all the time.

50
00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.725
That to me would be a little bit of overkill.

51
00:02:04.905 --> 00:02:07.165
But this is something kind of like feeling the vibe

52
00:02:07.265 --> 00:02:09.285
of the author and what they want out

53
00:02:09.285 --> 00:02:10.725
of given sections of the passage.

54
00:02:11.155 --> 00:02:14.445
It's all kind of one big cauldron that you're brewing

55
00:02:14.545 --> 00:02:17.525
as you're going because you know, newsflash

56
00:02:17.635 --> 00:02:20.805
what the author wants to achieve as in their purpose, like

57
00:02:20.805 --> 00:02:24.245
what they want to put forward is often quite similar to

58
00:02:24.245 --> 00:02:25.525
what they put forward, right?

59
00:02:25.635 --> 00:02:28.805
That like, oh, what's my purpose of this paragraph?

60
00:02:28.805 --> 00:02:31.285
Oh, well the purpose was to offer a counter example.

61
00:02:31.595 --> 00:02:35.285
Okay, what did this paragraph function as a counter example?

62
00:02:36.145 --> 00:02:37.765
You know, it's really not that deep,

63
00:02:37.795 --> 00:02:39.125
it's really not that complicated.

64
00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:43.245
But there are some small nuances that we of course want

65
00:02:43.245 --> 00:02:44.405
to keep track of, which is, you know,

66
00:02:44.405 --> 00:02:46.845
what they want is not always how well something

67
00:02:47.365 --> 00:02:48.565
actually functions, it's just

68
00:02:48.565 --> 00:02:50.205
that the two tend to be quite similar.

69
00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:53.565
Um, and an organization question it's grouped in with, um,

70
00:02:53.715 --> 00:02:56.685
with function because it is essentially a collection

71
00:02:56.705 --> 00:02:58.405
of functions in the correct answer.

72
00:02:58.625 --> 00:03:00.325
An organization question asks you

73
00:03:00.385 --> 00:03:04.245
to tell us all the major structural functions

74
00:03:04.245 --> 00:03:05.725
that occurred throughout the passage.

75
00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:08.805
For instance, it'll list like, oh, introducing a body

76
00:03:08.825 --> 00:03:12.365
of knowledge and then, um, offering a claim,

77
00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:15.685
introducing three examples to support said claim,

78
00:03:15.825 --> 00:03:19.365
and then, um, offering a qualification on the claim.

79
00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:21.885
And so that would be potentially the organization

80
00:03:21.885 --> 00:03:25.605
of the entire passage on um, the level of functions,

81
00:03:25.655 --> 00:03:29.005
which is just like we were saying, those, what those pieces

82
00:03:29.025 --> 00:03:32.045
of text are doing in the context of the author's project.

83
00:03:32.385 --> 00:03:34.925
And so we wanna be able to speak in that abstract way,

84
00:03:35.115 --> 00:03:36.365
just like method questions

85
00:03:36.665 --> 00:03:39.645
and on function organization questions, it's quite crucial

86
00:03:39.905 --> 00:03:41.365
to really come up with a mission

87
00:03:41.665 --> 00:03:43.165
that's really like a good mission,

88
00:03:43.235 --> 00:03:45.845
just like method in the question stem that

89
00:03:46.285 --> 00:03:48.005
whenever you, um, are in the question stem

90
00:03:48.005 --> 00:03:50.285
and you see it's functional organization, definitely go back

91
00:03:50.305 --> 00:03:51.965
and follow the rules of specific reference

92
00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:53.805
where you read a little above and a little below

93
00:03:54.105 --> 00:03:56.845
and you ask yourself, okay, what did this do?

94
00:03:57.595 --> 00:04:00.845
Like? And if you don't know, you need to broaden your scope,

95
00:04:01.185 --> 00:04:03.685
um, because you need to be able to say it

96
00:04:03.865 --> 00:04:05.285
before you go into the answers

97
00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:07.165
and start to get incepted by a lot

98
00:04:07.165 --> 00:04:08.645
of the answer choices ideas of what it did.

99
00:04:08.985 --> 00:04:12.685
Um, and if you want a little bit of extra help, one good,

100
00:04:13.105 --> 00:04:15.845
um, good habit you can fall into is

101
00:04:16.025 --> 00:04:18.805
to sometimes quip about the function

102
00:04:18.985 --> 00:04:20.645
of various aspects of the passage.

103
00:04:20.985 --> 00:04:24.485
So let's just say I'm reading a passage, uh, about plastics.

104
00:04:24.485 --> 00:04:25.805
It's like plastics are bad.

105
00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:28.085
And then, uh, that's like the major claim

106
00:04:28.085 --> 00:04:29.885
that's a say introduced in the first paragraph.

107
00:04:30.005 --> 00:04:32.245
I get to the next paragraph and it's like,

108
00:04:32.545 --> 00:04:35.125
and as we see with microplastics, you know,

109
00:04:35.125 --> 00:04:36.845
we're really hurting human health here.

110
00:04:37.385 --> 00:04:39.765
And I could immediately say, am my quip alright?

111
00:04:39.905 --> 00:04:42.325
So this seems like a reason why plastics are bad,

112
00:04:42.785 --> 00:04:45.005
or Here's an example of why plastics are bad.

113
00:04:45.425 --> 00:04:48.445
And when I say that I'm not only equipping upwards,

114
00:04:48.445 --> 00:04:51.805
you know, my patented equipping upwards hand motion, um,

115
00:04:52.465 --> 00:04:53.725
I'm clipping upwards, which is good

116
00:04:53.725 --> 00:04:55.085
because I'm incorporating earlier parts

117
00:04:55.085 --> 00:04:56.365
of the passage that helps my memory.

118
00:04:56.665 --> 00:04:58.485
I'm interrelating ideas allowing the passage

119
00:04:58.585 --> 00:05:00.205
to be in conversation with itself.

120
00:05:00.505 --> 00:05:04.085
Um, but then I'm also inadvertently getting the function

121
00:05:04.085 --> 00:05:05.205
there really naturally.

122
00:05:05.545 --> 00:05:07.325
Um, and that's why I don't want this

123
00:05:07.325 --> 00:05:08.805
to be this like rote mechanical thing.

124
00:05:09.245 --> 00:05:13.245
I just want the function to naturally kind of imbue itself

125
00:05:13.795 --> 00:05:15.525
into a lot of your quips along

126
00:05:15.525 --> 00:05:16.805
with other functions of quips.

127
00:05:16.805 --> 00:05:18.245
This isn't of course, like the only quip

128
00:05:18.245 --> 00:05:22.045
that ever quip on rc, but it can definitely be a useful one

129
00:05:22.045 --> 00:05:24.965
because it will keep you thinking in that structural way

130
00:05:25.145 --> 00:05:26.165
as you're going through the passage

131
00:05:26.345 --> 00:05:28.485
and really ingraining that content for yourself.

132
00:05:28.625 --> 00:05:30.885
So it can be really helpful. And so let's go

133
00:05:30.885 --> 00:05:34.605
through just a brief example of like how functions

134
00:05:34.605 --> 00:05:35.805
of text could work.

135
00:05:36.265 --> 00:05:38.205
Um, and so lemme go through this one.

136
00:05:38.205 --> 00:05:39.405
Alright, let's say we have a claim

137
00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:42.445
and it's the modern crisis in recycling is due to a lack

138
00:05:42.445 --> 00:05:44.725
of public education on recycling, apparently.

139
00:05:44.725 --> 00:05:46.725
Like people don't really know anything about recycling

140
00:05:46.725 --> 00:05:48.445
and they put things in the wrong bins and whatnot.

141
00:05:48.585 --> 00:05:51.045
And so let's say that was introduced in our first paragraph,

142
00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:52.405
and it seems like a claim.

143
00:05:52.405 --> 00:05:54.125
The authors introduced stuff about recycling

144
00:05:54.125 --> 00:05:55.685
and then they lead to this and it's like this

145
00:05:55.685 --> 00:05:56.805
beautifully structured thing.

146
00:05:57.145 --> 00:05:58.725
And then in the next paragraph,

147
00:05:59.275 --> 00:06:02.845
well the modern crisis on recycling is actually due

148
00:06:02.845 --> 00:06:04.805
to outdated machinery and recycling plants.

149
00:06:05.705 --> 00:06:08.965
As soon as you read that, even though there isn't any kind

150
00:06:08.965 --> 00:06:10.925
of like, however on the other hand

151
00:06:10.945 --> 00:06:13.685
or whatever kind of thing, you can immediately tell, okay,

152
00:06:13.835 --> 00:06:16.885
this is going against the idea that the crisis is

153
00:06:16.885 --> 00:06:18.325
because of a lack of education, right?

154
00:06:18.555 --> 00:06:21.005
It's giving an alternative explanation, um,

155
00:06:21.385 --> 00:06:23.365
to the claim, um, cited above.

156
00:06:23.555 --> 00:06:25.045
It's like, oh, the claim cited above.

157
00:06:25.045 --> 00:06:27.205
It was trying to explain the certain problem about why we

158
00:06:27.205 --> 00:06:28.685
have this modern crisis in recycling.

159
00:06:28.785 --> 00:06:30.565
Now here's a different way to think about it.

160
00:06:30.745 --> 00:06:34.365
And so all of the ways that I'm phrasing how this, um,

161
00:06:34.465 --> 00:06:37.085
new phrase could work about the outdated machinery,

162
00:06:37.255 --> 00:06:40.205
those could all be answers to a function question on

163
00:06:40.235 --> 00:06:41.285
what the function of

164
00:06:41.285 --> 00:06:44.605
that phrase was in the overall project of the passage.

165
00:06:45.105 --> 00:06:46.765
Um, one more example.

166
00:06:47.105 --> 00:06:50.445
So let's say we have a, uh, we have a claim

167
00:06:50.445 --> 00:06:52.845
that plastic is really bad, and then

168
00:06:53.705 --> 00:06:56.325
in our next few paragraphs we're like, yes,

169
00:06:56.665 --> 00:06:58.445
here's some examples that support this claim.

170
00:06:58.585 --> 00:07:02.085
Um, the oceans are ruined, the plastic uses petroleum

171
00:07:02.085 --> 00:07:03.245
to be made and you know,

172
00:07:03.245 --> 00:07:04.285
microplastics are hurting

173
00:07:04.285 --> 00:07:05.445
our health, or something like that.

174
00:07:05.445 --> 00:07:06.765
And it's like, okay, these are all

175
00:07:06.765 --> 00:07:08.005
reasons why plastic is bad.

176
00:07:08.205 --> 00:07:10.085
I could be whipping or at least thinking about that

177
00:07:10.385 --> 00:07:12.245
as I'm going, but remember,

178
00:07:12.245 --> 00:07:14.525
this is also not a replacement for the translation.

179
00:07:14.835 --> 00:07:16.325
Knowing that these are examples

180
00:07:16.325 --> 00:07:18.245
that support plastics are bad is not the same thing

181
00:07:18.245 --> 00:07:19.325
as knowing what the examples are.

182
00:07:19.585 --> 00:07:22.845
Um, but let's say I get to my last paragraph and they say,

183
00:07:23.025 --> 00:07:25.165
but plastics make modern healthcare possible.

184
00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:28.845
And, you know, lifesaving outcomes depend on

185
00:07:28.845 --> 00:07:30.165
single use plastics.

186
00:07:30.625 --> 00:07:33.485
So what was that freeze now doing? Right?

187
00:07:33.715 --> 00:07:36.445
It's offering maybe a qualification on our claim.

188
00:07:36.635 --> 00:07:40.085
It's like, oh, actually our plastics all bad, right?

189
00:07:40.235 --> 00:07:42.485
This sounds like a positive thing about plastics.

190
00:07:42.625 --> 00:07:44.405
And so perhaps it's a counter example.

191
00:07:44.405 --> 00:07:46.085
Perhaps it's offering a qualification,

192
00:07:46.545 --> 00:07:48.445
but it's certainly not the same thing

193
00:07:48.465 --> 00:07:50.165
as those other examples that were given

194
00:07:50.265 --> 00:07:54.205
before that were actually like pro plastic is bad.

195
00:07:54.625 --> 00:07:56.925
Um, and so let's just talk for a second about

196
00:07:56.925 --> 00:07:58.965
how this manifests with organization questions.

197
00:07:58.965 --> 00:08:00.645
Essentially with organization questions,

198
00:08:00.645 --> 00:08:03.245
you're just gonna see a list of functions in each answer.

199
00:08:03.505 --> 00:08:06.005
And I really like to do these questions piecemeal

200
00:08:06.135 --> 00:08:07.485
where I just read

201
00:08:07.485 --> 00:08:10.325
and translate the first piece of each answer choice.

202
00:08:10.705 --> 00:08:15.445
Um, and when I see one, uh, function listed, like let's say

203
00:08:15.635 --> 00:08:18.245
that this imaginary passage, it starts with an introduction

204
00:08:18.385 --> 00:08:19.725
to a discipline, you know,

205
00:08:19.725 --> 00:08:21.885
maybe it's recycling, um, and then a claim.

206
00:08:22.265 --> 00:08:25.645
And then let's say answer D it starts off

207
00:08:25.645 --> 00:08:27.685
with introduces two points of view

208
00:08:27.785 --> 00:08:30.045
and it's like, yo, there were no two points of view.

209
00:08:30.345 --> 00:08:32.565
So as soon as that bell rings of like, ooh,

210
00:08:32.565 --> 00:08:36.005
something problematic on answer DI will immediately read the

211
00:08:36.005 --> 00:08:39.045
rest of answer d um, to vet the rest of it

212
00:08:39.045 --> 00:08:40.445
and see if it totally goes off the rails

213
00:08:40.445 --> 00:08:42.845
or if it's, you know, actually, maybe I was being sensitive

214
00:08:42.985 --> 00:08:45.205
and it really isn't that bad, or whatever it might be.

215
00:08:45.665 --> 00:08:49.645
And if it's off the rails, I knock D out, then you know, I,

216
00:08:49.765 --> 00:08:51.925
I go to EI read the first part, maybe it's passable.

217
00:08:51.925 --> 00:08:54.845
Alright, now I go and do all the second parts. Um,

218
00:08:55.245 --> 00:08:56.605
I really like doing this just

219
00:08:56.605 --> 00:08:58.645
because it allows you to compare like with like,

220
00:08:58.865 --> 00:09:00.525
of like all the second pieces

221
00:09:01.065 --> 00:09:03.205
of the answer, all the third pieces.

222
00:09:03.625 --> 00:09:05.725
Um, and then since you focus on the answer

223
00:09:05.735 --> 00:09:07.565
after it kind of rings that bell of like,

224
00:09:07.565 --> 00:09:09.005
there's something a little bit problematic here,

225
00:09:09.275 --> 00:09:10.525
then you can focus on it

226
00:09:10.525 --> 00:09:12.165
and really know that it's wrong before you get rid of it.

227
00:09:12.345 --> 00:09:14.845
And so that is one way you can take function

228
00:09:14.945 --> 00:09:16.645
and turn it into organization.

229
00:09:16.985 --> 00:09:18.925
Um, since organization is just a lot

230
00:09:18.925 --> 00:09:20.285
of functions all listed in the answer

231
00:09:20.305 --> 00:09:22.285
and you of course wanna get rid of an answer choice

232
00:09:22.515 --> 00:09:25.405
that lists functions that did not occur in the answer.

233
00:09:25.825 --> 00:09:27.645
Um, and so I hope that this was helpful.

234
00:09:27.845 --> 00:09:29.685
I hope that you're busy porting your

235
00:09:30.275 --> 00:09:33.485
logical reasoning skills over to RC and I will see you soon.

